    verification rejectIsHumanJudgedWhereAnAcceptanceIsReviewGate : Test { :>> id = "be9a3705-5427-4137-b08b-92c39c1ea250"; :>> title = "Sprint 702 review gate — three passes per D0172"; :>> createdAt = "2026-09-13"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Three passes (D0172). Correctness: refuse_ai_judgment is the same function accept, judge-set, rebind and confirmation results call, with the same short-circuit outside a model tree (unit-test scratch) and the same actors.sysml presence test - so reject gains exactly accept's guarantee, no more and no less; the call precedes the read so a refused rejection touches nothing. The rule predicate resultJudgedByHuman(suffix) resolves {name}{suffix}R1 and reject_decision writes {decision}RejectR1, so the rule reads the event the write produces. Honesty: the guard keeps its name confirmation-authenticity and adds the rejected branch under it rather than a new guard, because the control is one claim (a human verdict is human-judged) over two statuses; an absent rejectionAuthenticityRule warns NOT ADOPTED like the accepted one does, never silently passes. Scope: the serve endpoint (serve.rs:941) already required a registered Person at the transport layer; it now also meets the write-layer refusal, which is the layer that binds regardless of caller. The command-layer channel refusals (verdict_channel_refusal) are unchanged."; }
